[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the application layer on the destination server (apache2 web service on srv1, 10.5.0.4). Network routing and L2/L3 connectivity between Office A and Office B are healthy.

Mechanism: From Office A (pc1: 10.1.0.100), ICMP reachability to 10.5.0.4 works with low RTT and zero loss, proving the inter-office path is intact. However, HTTP application checks fail: curl from pc1 to http://10.5.0.4 returns code=000/rc=7 (failed to connect). On srv1, netstat shows no listener on TCP/80, only DNS (53), and systemctl reports apache2 “inactive (dead)”. Thus, user “can’t get to the server” is an application-service outage, not a transport issue.

Localization: srv1 (Office B), interface eth0 (10.5.0.4/24) is up and reachable; the apache2 daemon is stopped, so TCP/80 is closed.

Root cause: The apache2 service on srv1 was left stopped after last night’s change, leaving no HTTP listener on 10.5.0.4.

Corrective fix:
- On srv1, start and enable the web service: systemctl start apache2 && systemctl enable apache2.
- Verify it is listening: netstat -tuln | grep ':80' and curl http://10.5.0.4 from pc1.
- If it fails to start, review /var/log/apache2/error.log and recent change configs, then revert or correct syntax.
No network changes are required; the inter-office connectivity is functioning as evidenced by successful ping between offices.

=== END ===